[1] square-in-ball containment (circumradius s/sqrt2)
    r=1.500000: admissible 2 < s < sqrt2*r = (2, 2.121320) -> NONEMPTY
    r=1.414214: admissible 2 < s < sqrt2*r = (2, 2.000000) -> NONEMPTY
    r=2.000000: admissible 2 < s < sqrt2*r = (2, 2.828427) -> NONEMPTY
    r=4.000000: admissible 2 < s < sqrt2*r = (2, 5.656854) -> NONEMPTY
    WEAKEST.md's printed '4 < s < r/sqrt2' needs r > 4*sqrt2 = 5.656854, contradicting its own r > sqrt2 = 1.414214; correct line is '2 < s < sqrt2*r'
[2] disk form: needed inradius = component-cover mesh = 1+o(1), so the radius threshold is 1
    (paper's rounded mesh 2 would give 2; m12 Lemma 5.1 states inradius(Q) > mesh)
[3] Prop. 1 window: a disk of radius r>1 avoiding x fits in B(x,rho) iff 2r < rho
    rho=1.5000: no contradiction -> rho <= 2
    rho=1.9990: no contradiction -> rho <= 2
    rho=2.0000: no contradiction -> rho <= 2
    rho=2.0010: CONTRADICTION with density -> rho <= 2
[4] L = (6/5)Z^2   [OPEN-ball convention, corrected this block]
    covering radius a/sqrt2 = 0.848528137 <= 1 -> 1-dense (density theorem not violated)
    nearest-neighbour distance = 1.2; isolation radius r* = 1.2 (= a, NOT a/2 = 0.6)
    (ISO)(r) holds for r <= 6/5, fails for r > 6/5; it refutes r > sqrt2 = 1.4142 since 6/5 < sqrt2: True
    exact: (6/5)^2 = 36/25 <= 2 : True
[5] L = {0,(1/50,0)}: |L cap B(0,1.5)| = 2 finite -> (W); isolation radii 1/100
    -> (W) is strictly weaker than 'isolated point with gap > 1'
[6] lattice r* = a (open ball excludes the neighbours at distance exactly a)
    L = 1.0Z^2: r* = 1.0, packing radius 0.5, covering radius 0.707107 -> 1-dense True
    L = 1.2Z^2: r* = 1.2, packing radius 0.6, covering radius 0.848528 -> 1-dense True
    L = 1.5Z^2: r* = 1.5, packing radius 0.75, covering radius 1.060660 -> 1-dense False
    a = 1  : r* = 1   -> (ISO)(r) FAILS for every r > 1 while 1-dense (refutes gap>1)
    a = 6/5: r* = 1.2 -> (ISO)(r) HOLDS for r in (1,6/5]; refutes only r > 6/5, e.g. r > sqrt2
[7] L = {0} u U_k S(0, 3k/2): (W) holds, (W2) fails
    |L cap B(0,1.2)| = 1 (nearest circle at radius 1.5) -> (W) with R = 1.2
      |y|=0.0: dist(y,L)=0.000 <= 1 (1-dense); nearest circle within 2 -> |L cap B(y,2)| infinite
      |y|=0.3: dist(y,L)=0.300 <= 1 (1-dense); nearest circle within 2 -> |L cap B(y,2)| infinite
      |y|=0.74: dist(y,L)=0.740 <= 1 (1-dense); nearest circle within 2 -> |L cap B(y,2)| infinite
      |y|=1.0: dist(y,L)=0.500 <= 1 (1-dense); nearest circle within 2 -> |L cap B(y,2)| infinite
      |y|=2.3: dist(y,L)=0.700 <= 1 (1-dense); nearest circle within 2 -> |L cap B(y,2)| infinite
      |y|=7.7: dist(y,L)=0.200 <= 1 (1-dense); nearest circle within 2 -> |L cap B(y,2)| infinite
    radii are 1.5 apart, so every y lies within 0.75 of a circle and every radius-2 ball cuts
    one in an arc => (W2) FAILS for every y, while (W) holds
[9] weak-limit countermodel (12 sectors, T = {3,4,5})
    <b_0,b_1> = 0/12 = 0; || b_0 - b_1 ||^2 = 2 - 2*0 = 2
    <b_0,b_2> = 0/12 = 0; || b_0 - b_2 ||^2 = 2 - 2*0 = 2
    <b_0,b_3> = 12/12 = 1; || b_0 - b_3 ||^2 = 2 - 2*1 = 0
    => the family has only 3 distinct members, so a constant (hence L^2-Cauchy) subsequence
       exists; the illustration is about the INFERENCE below, not about non-compactness
    convex average of 3 consecutive = (1/3,1/3,1/3); all three coordinates > 0,
    so a WEAK limit would have |P| >= 3 at the accumulation point. Traces are STRONG
    limits (trace_realization: sampleCode -> b in L^2), so this inference is invalid.
    => 'singleton palettes pass to the limit' is false; only 'the limit singleton is IN the palette'
[10] Prop. 4 conclusion, as a label statement
    V=[0, 1, 2] V'=[0, 1, 2]: T cap T' = [3, 4, 5] (nonempty -> OK)
    V=[0, 1, 2] V'=[3, 4, 5]: T cap T' = [] -> EMPTY, excluded by Prop. 4
    equivalently V u V' != [6]: a missing label is shared by the two triangles
    ordered pairs (V,V') with V u V' = [6]: 20 of 400 = 1/20
    unordered such pairs: 10 of 210
[11] m3 (k=7) consistency check of Prop. A
    triangle labels {0,2,3}, complement T = [1, 4, 5, 6], |T| = 4 = k-3 = 4
    m3: the unit circle about q_n lies outside the gadget W (radius ~1/2 about q_n in the
    far field), so its colours are the four background labels {1,4,5,6} = T. Prop. A holds
    with equality, and this is exactly why k >= 7 can carry four outsiders and k = 6 cannot.
[13] Rademacher counterexample (Omega = {0,1}^3, uniform)
    k=0, l=1: P(u_k != u_l) = 4/8 = 1/2; ||u_k-u_l||^2 = 2*1/2 = 1; P(|u_k-u_l| >= 1) = 1/2
    k=0, l=2: P(u_k != u_l) = 4/8 = 1/2; ||u_k-u_l||^2 = 2*1/2 = 1; P(|u_k-u_l| >= 1) = 1/2
    k=1, l=2: P(u_k != u_l) = 4/8 = 1/2; ||u_k-u_l||^2 = 2*1/2 = 1; P(|u_k-u_l| >= 1) = 1/2
    => pairwise L^2 distance 1 for every pair, and L^0 distance 1/2: no Cauchy subsequence.
    So the set of one-hot (T-valued) fields is NOT compact in L^2 or in measure:
    'closed and bounded in L^0' does not give compactness on a nonatomic space.
[14] admissibility bookkeeping
    Lean: TypicalSampling c y r := 0 < r and (for ae t) Typical (y + r*direction t),
    Typical z := DensityOne {w : c w = c z} z.  IsTrace x b := (x,1,b) in closure(sampleSet).
    Lean has ae_typicalSampling: for fixed r > 0, a.e. y is admissible (a.e.-in-CENTRE).
    The per-centre form needed here is different: the non-typical set N is plane-null and
    the circle is an isometry of angular measure, so (x,1) is admissible for EVERY x.
    That is paper-level (Lemma 4.3 + absolute continuity), not the Lean a.e. statement.
    Hence admissibility is NOT the gap; convergence of the codes is.
